A||||47||Mach10||An Editorial: ||||3||
Z||000000||Mach10||10-14-2001||02:02 PM||||So America has gone to war. Retaliation for a sneak attack that claimed thousands of lives. Big Bad USA, technologically one of, if not the most advanced nations in the world. Gone to war against a country made up of goat-farmers, sheep-herders and peasants. Oh, and a large paramilitary extremist group bent on the destruction of western society for any number of percieved injuries.<P>The media has been spouting "America at War" messages since that faithful day on Sept 11th. People have compared it to Pearl Harbor. Without a doubt it was a direct attack on the States. Was it an act of War? My opinion, yes it was. Some people point out that it was the USA's own fault, that their "foreign policy" is what had caused the deaths of almost 7,000 people that day. I'm sorry, but WHAT did the US do to deserve an attack like that?<P>Let's just jump over to Afghanistan for a moment. The ruling power at the moment is the Taliban. Ultra-hardcore extremists to the bone, they preach their own version of the holy qu'ran. Instead of peace and enlightenment--which is what mohammed was try to do-- they preach repression, violence, and xenophobia. They had started out quelling the in-fighting and civil war with brute force. hundreds of thousands of teenagers made up their army. They get their kicks now by rigorous enforcement of their value system. Savage beatings of people found erring from their twisted path to paradise. Executions for crimes allegedly committed. Hey, if they were not guilty, Allah would not permit their execution, now would he? Sounds to me like the Salem Witch Trials. People are being condemned and executed on heresay...<P>So who's the real monsters? The US? With their questionable foreign policies, sometimes going so far as to put economic sanctions on smaller countries? Oh the HORROR. Or is it the Taliban, with thier inhumane and cruel acts made in the name of God?<P>At this moment, US forces are pounding Taliban installations. It's not much of a conquest, really. The biggest challenge the US has is to keep their bombs in line. To date, there have been few civilian casualties. There have been some, and this is both unavoidable and regrettable. The Taliban drones on about how the US is a terrorist nation. Pardon me, gentlemen, but the US declared WAR on you. The US has been engaging military targets. Some civilians were in the wrong place in the wrong time. So the terrorist organizations that slaughtered 7000 people were justified? <P>The terrorist networks have been feverishly pumping out propaganda. Watch the skies they say! More suicide planes to come! Anthrax is breaking out all over the country. The result? The population is terrified. Maybe it's just me, but, isn't this what they want? What kind of retard lets the target know how he's going to be hit? Sounds to me like someone's blowing smoke up the USA's proverbial ass. <P>The Taliban is busy trying to rouse the sleepy muslim population (all 350,000,000 of them) into going to war with the US. That will be sticky. That appears to me to have been the intent of the terrorists, and now appears to be the intent of the Taliban. The worst part of it is, if the Middle East polarizes against the west, the west will do the same. Sorry boys, but you are MAJORLY outnumbered. But Pakistan has nukes. I can't help but wonder if that is the intent? Spark a nuclear war? The middle east stands a good chance of coming out on top. The world's nuclear arsenal is aimed at major cities and military installations all over the civilized world. Nothing will be heading towards the middle east. Pakistan will be vaporized, but that leaves a lot left. <P>In the meantime, we watch the news. Grainy starscope videos of whitish streaky things slamming into gray streaky things and blowing up. The US tries (valiantly, in my opinion) to avoid civilian casualties. The Taliban trumpets on about injustice, and how the US will pay dearly for the western world's crimes against islam. My question to the leaders of the Taliban is; Who will pay for the crimes against us? <P><BR><B>These writings are my own take on what's going on</B><BR>||||reg||3
Z||000001||hugh||10-14-2001||02:20 PM||||You have put into words what a lot of people feel!Thanks!||||reg||14
Z||000002||Mach10||10-14-2001||02:41 PM||||I HAVE to add a few things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Is anyone else watching the hilarity that makes up the Taliban? I swear, if arrogance was a crime punishable by death...<P>The Taliban is telling the rest of the world that it is in negotiations with the Northern Alliances, trying to get them to band up against the US. <P>What did one of the military leaders have to say about that? <BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Commenting on a proposal from the Taliban that opposition fighters join them in their war against the United States, Abdullah said that, on the contrary, Taliban forces were defecting to the opposition. <P>``The Taliban have been giving us surprises all the time by issuing statements,'' he said. ``It is the people of the Taliban themselves who are defecting... not vice versa,'' he said. <BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is anyone else reminded of the schoolyard bully that's been beaten black and blue and is screaming insults and threats as he runs away? ||||reg||10
Z||000003||maryjane||10-14-2001||02:45 PM||||I don't think people here are terrified. Concerned, but not what I know as terror. Taliban has been trying to use the press a lot more tho, taking cnn into only the civilian areas hit for instance. That's why I always said, if we're going to fight a war, fight it. Don't wimp around till you lose support of people here & abroad. Turn the dogs loose, let em run, do damage control later. It could have been over by now. I'm sorry, but I'm beginning to see Vietnam all over this thing. This isn't war, it's politics, on an international scale. This things going to blow up in our face (the world's) if we don't get it over & done with before winter sets in. Thousands freeze & starve there every winter, this time it will be unjustly blamed on us.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||10-14-2001||02:51 PM||||I should clarify... The panic is being caused by the Anthrax breakouts, regardless of source. <BR>Yes, the tree-hugging hippy freaks are back out. ||||reg||7
Z||000005||maryjane||10-14-2001||03:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>I HAVE to add a few things  <P>is anyone else reminded of the schoolyard bully that's been beaten black and blue and is screaming insults and threats as he runs away? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That's my point. In war, you don't beat the enemy black & blue & leave em yelling at you. You kill em & be done with it. Make the price they pay high enough and they'll shut up, & will remember it too. Just wondering what the families of the WTC dead think of our concern for collatoral damages. We made this mistake in Iraq, Saddam's still mouthing off.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||California Kid||10-14-2001||03:13 PM||||I agree with maryjane on this one. The whole situation appears to be getting out of control because we aren't hitting them hard enough. This whole thing is bound to get nastier than Viet Nam ever was. It also appears that too many groups are using this as an opportunity to step up on the soap box and complain about our actions, which is just stirring everybody else up. We are currently in an age of chaos, which will lead to many deaths at home and abroad.<P>Wish everyone luck on this one, glad I did my stretch in Viet Nam, just worried about my son's getting caught in this mess.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||10-14-2001||03:26 PM||||Actually, I was getting those vibes BEFORE the attacks. The Taliban talks big, but nothing ever comes of it. I wonder if they will learn that they can bully their own people, but that the rest of the world can give a ****  less what they say?<P>As for the hit hard, clean up later, I'm not sure what I feel about that. There are merits, certainly. I'm kinda for the approach that they have now. But then, more firepower concentrated at the military targetst that they have now would certainly not hurt us...||||reg||3
Z||000008||maryjane||10-14-2001||04:09 PM||||Mach10, you have a PM||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||10-14-2001||04:24 PM||||MJ had a great idea: <P>For all you with military experience, or strategy gaming experience, how would you carry out the campaign? <P>PLEASE! No "Just nuke 'em" posts... They are getting OLD.<P>The goal is to bring the responsible people to justice, and exact righteous retaliation on the Taliban Military with MINIMAL Civilian casualties... Does anyone have a map of the theater, and a list of what's being deployed? Don't need positions, just some "game peices" ||||reg||10
Z||000010||fierohoho||10-14-2001||05:09 PM||||Ya know, I think an important issue that I only saw the media touch on lightly was the fact that the Taliban's main source of income,(remember they live in a big sand box and the last time I checked they were not exporting landscape rock for new houses),is the production and export of Heroin.<P>They may say they are protecting themselves but if you ask me they are trying to protect their source of income.<P>With the entire country effectively blockaded the product is not going out and the $ is not comming in.<P>This will make them a little desparate I think.<P>As for the war, I think that as it is reported to us and I have time to think about it, the strategie is very well thought out.<P>Don't rush, time is on our side.<P>They limit ground forces so no freindly fire casualties and just use air drops of bombs then food.<P>I even saw a news report that they were thinking of droping those wind up radios,(no batteries) so the "people" could get the news on what is happening and why.<P>I liked that fact they took out the main radio station there and now fly a transmitting plane broadcasting on the old frequency and delivering news and info I just wish the media could use another word for it than propoganda, makes it sound too communist.<P>The big problem as I see it in the war is now the bad guys have retreated into the mountains and are dug in which will make it a little hard to route then out but we should be able to get em I think.<P>My main concern is about who is next when we're done with Afghanistan, any thoughts?<P>I'll do a rant pause for now <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Typical regime in power that dictates what you do and when you do it, we've seen this before and we'll see it again, hopefully we will be able to stop it before it gets too big next time.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||10-14-2001||05:59 PM||||Another thing to ponder:<P>If they DO catch Osama Bin Laden, what should the US do with him? Seriously...<P>I'm in favor of a full, televised, and above all fair trial in a US Court. Try him as you would an America, according to US law, and punish likewise. ||||reg||4
Z||000012||Raydar||10-14-2001||08:08 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Another thing to ponder:<P>If they DO catch Osama Bin Laden, what should the US do with him? Seriously...<P>I'm in favor of a full, televised, and above all fair trial in a US Court. Try him as you would an America, according to US law, and punish likewise. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>People keep stating this, in one form or another. <BR>Why try him? He's NOT a US citizen. He doesn't have ANY rights as a US citizen. He has already openly stated that he has declared war on us. Fsck him. We ought to bury him under one of his mountains. Or he and his cronies should just quietly be made to disappear.  <P>And that lack of rights as a US citizen ought to be policy for <I>anyone</I> who is in this country illegally. Not just people of Middle Eastern descent. They should be automatically deported, at the very least. If there is any suspicion of criminal activity, they should be imprisoned until we get around to dealing with them. This policy wouldn't have to be in effect for more than a few months, before people would start paying attention and quit taking advantage (or worse) of us.<P>And I <I>am</I> sorry that some civilians got killed or injured. But, oh well.<BR>We didn't target them. It <I>was</I> an accident. <BR>If they want to whine about civilian casualties, we should remind them of the 6000 or so, of our own. People that are trying to promote their own agenda seem to conveniently overlook that *little* detail.<P>[/rant]<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000013||Doni Hagan||10-14-2001||08:28 PM||||Although there's no debate whatsoever that the Taliban is a sponsor of terrorism and that Ben Laden is a global PITA, it seems that the western press is concentrating far too heavily upon the Middle Eastern component of the situation. If one is to define "terrorism" in the strictist sense then the possibility of eliminating it by focusing on the Middle East will probably prove futile in the long run. <P>Prior to 9/11/01, the worst terrorist attack on this nation was committed not by an Arabian religious zealot but by Timothy McVeigh, a good ole' American boy. <P>America loves a villain we can all concentrate our anger towards. However, we must be careful not to look for the easy bad guy and overlook the more covert threats. For example, is it rational to believe that the same Ben Laden who committed the "large scale" WTC atrocity is now mailing little blue envelopes of Anthrax to media offices? When the fervor calms down and the dust clears a little, I for one will not be the least bit surprized to find that someone a lot closer to home is responsible for the recent outbreak. <P>Get Ben Laden? Certainly.. Depose the Taliban, Of course, as long as we have no desire to rule the Afghani people ourselves. But will that be the end of it?....... I seriously doubt it.  <p>[This message has been edited by Doni Hagan (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||loafer87gt||10-14-2001||08:45 PM||||What I can't believe is the sheer stupidity of the Afghanny people. First off, after the attacks they are celebrating in the streets praising OBL and the attacks on the US. Now that the bombing attacks have been underway, they are claiming the US is the wrong do-er, and that there is no reason why the US should be attacking them because OBL is not in Afghanistan. Then during this whole thing the Taliban is more or less admitting that they know where OBL is and that they would be willing to hand him over to a third party country. Meanwhile, countless droves of civilians are signing up to fight the "evil powers" of the US, supporting a leader who has made continued threats to the US and is almost solely responsible for their plight. I know this may sound overly harsh, but I say don't draw a line between Taliban targets and Afghani civilians. Once the Taliban is wiped out, there is going to be more than enough fanatical civilians wanting to take their place in the cockpits of next wave of planes headed toward the US. I'm getting a little tired of how the media keeps reporting on the civilian causualties incurred during the military campaign now being waged, well, that tends to happen during times of war. And the total of 200 "innocent civilians" killed during the strikes still hasn't came anywhere near the 6500 that perished on Sept. 11th.<p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||loafer87gt||10-14-2001||08:46 PM||||oops, double post<p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||10-14-2001||08:55 PM||||By treating him to a fair and equitable trial, you put the US in a good light. <BR>By televising it, he cannot become a martyr, because the evidence is open to the public.<BR>By giving him a sentence that applies to all of the rest of Americans gives even more strenghth to the "Land of the Free" argument<P>So yes, I DO think you should treat him like you would an american. The most fanatical won't buy any of it, but 95% of the rest of the world will, and the US will come out on top. Plus, we can pay 2.95 to watch the fncker fry like a pork sausage.<P>As for the Afghani people, I would call them ignorant, not stupid. They believe what they are told to believe. Remember, that by demolishing the Taliban, you are attacking their clergy. That won't make you popular. <P>A nice touch the US is making is bombing the cr@p out of an area, then immediately parachuting supplies into the craters. Makes the US-Terrorist view a little harder to swallow  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000017||California Kid||10-14-2001||09:27 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Doni Hagan:<BR>[B]Get Ben Laden? Certainly.. Depose the Taliban, Of course, as long as we have no desire to rule the Afghani people ourselves. But will that be the end of it?....... I seriously doubt it.<BR>********************************************<P>Bingo!!!! Give that man a cigar!!!<P>Anyone paying attention to the world news has probably noticed these nuts are all over the planet. Seems lately the right wing religious nuts who don't fear taking anothers life, including their own, are about to start a chain reaction in the name of their god. I hope I'm wrong, but I'm not too excited about what will happen in the near future if NATO doesn't get all these other countries on the "bad wagon" to eradicate these type of individuals. Sure some innocent people are going to get killed, we already lost a huge number.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Doni Hagan||10-15-2001||12:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B><BR>I hope I'm wrong, but I'm not too excited about what will happen in the near future if NATO doesn't get all these other countries on the "bad wagon" to eradicate these type of individuals.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Theoretically, that sounds good. However, keep in mind that the "eradication" you refer to will ultimately include some of our own citizens. I'm not being sarcastic and, after living in Europe for a decade, wouldn't even dream of living anywhere else. <P>Having said that, you must admit that there are people born, raised and residing in <BR>this country who wouldn't know an Afghani citizen from an Afghan Hound that are quite pissed off at our government, rightly or wrongly. Is the person that shoots a doctor at an abortion clinic or levels, say, a FederalBuilding in Oklahoma City any less a "terrorist" than someone who walks into a packed restaurant in Tel Aviv and blows himself and others to bits?<P>I'm just hoping that we, as a collective body, don't lose patience for what is sure to be a long and painful struggle.....if the job is seriously going to be completed. Like my granddad use to say "In for a penny, in for a pound." If you attempt a half-assed solution, we will certainly obtain half-assed results. And, considering the lives at stake, we can't afford anything half-assed. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||My7Fieros||10-15-2001||01:13 AM||||I often scratch my head and wonder about all the people (in the U.S.) who are so concerned about the "minimal civilian casualties in Afghanistan". Who cares? I, for one, do not. You have got to realize that, yes, even though they are "civilians", they do not hold the same values of life that (most) Americans do. <BR>Case in point....whens the last time you saw on tv Afghan people marching in the streets burning the American flag and chanting anti-U.S. comments? Answer: probobly today or yesterday. Now, when was the last time you saw American civilians marching and burning the Afgahnistan flag?....uh huh..I thought so.<BR>For those of you (you know who you are), who keep calling some people "right wing religious fanatics"...take a look at yourself. Why do you think you're so much better?<BR>This whole whiny tree hugging, save the whales, make love not war liberal garbage is <B>quickly</B> getting out of style. There will always be people who dont want war or dont want to retaliate blah blah blah. Hippies, you are cowards, period. You say you dont believe in "violence" or the like, yet the first time a gang banger kills/rapes/attacks, whatever, an innocent person, your the first ones to say that its ok because "he had a bad childhood" or "he's been kept down by society". <BR>Bring OBL to the U.S. and "put him on trial and treat him as a citizen..that way the U.S. will look better throughout the world" you say? <I>pshhhhh</I>...sure. Thats the absolute stupidest thing I think Ive ever heard. And btw, like I said before, this extreme liberal attitude is losing ground, and quickly. Most people in the U.S. arent too concerned w/ "the innocent civilian casualites in Afghanistan" or "bringing OBL to American justice (courts, blah blah blah). I've about had it up to my eyeballs with that kind of mentality. Go on and nick pick the facts until your eyes bleed to try to justify your feelings..you're only fooling yourself. Im happy w/ the way the U.S. is handling the situation...w/ the exception of dropping food on them.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>(p.s. note I didnt say "nuke 'em all either"). <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My7logo_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Doni Hagan||10-15-2001||10:37 AM||||I totally agree with "7 Fieros" regarding the uproar about a few civilian casualties. Show me ONE war fought since man has been able to whittle a sharp end on a stick in which the only people killed happened to have uniforms on. Civilian casualties are a by-product of war, particularly in instances where governments or regimes place their citizens either directly in the line of fire or follow a policy that's sure to get them bombed (like refusing to give up one a$$h@!@.)<P>This whole bombing exercise could've easily been avoided. Turn Ben Laden over and that's that...everybody out of the pool. It probably would not eliminate future terrorist attacks on our soil but the Afghani people wouldn't have to walk around looking up constantly. <P>If the media want to place blame for civilian casualties in Kabul, perhaps they should ask the people in New York City who should receive it. At least 6000 less civilians are available to answer the question. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Oreif||10-15-2001||12:07 PM||||OK, Here's a few things. First, The way we are "attacking" tha Taliban is about the only way you can do it from the air. Next you need to cut the money flow. Freezing accounts is a start but the drug money needs to stop. So eliminate the drugs. The next phase is the ground fight. Problem is, is the civilians will get involved. It falls under the psychology thing. Even though the Taliban treat the civilians like dogs, That is what they are use to. When someone outside tries to change it they get angry and oppose it. They would not be "terrorist" but they would still fight for the Taliban.<BR>So how would you tell who's on your side or the other? This was the same problem in Viet Nam. The "enemy" could walk right past you on a street and you wouldn't know it until the bomb went off.<P> Second, As for OBL, You cannot bring him here for a trial. He would most likely be sentenced to death. But like anyone else he'll have a chance to appeal and other legal tactics. This now opens the door for extorsion. Many fanatics will barter to get him released. Usually by kidnapping others. The way to get him is to have the trial in the middle east. When he is found guilty, he will be shot 12 hours later. It is the law of the middle east countries. But the Taliban cannot be part of the trial. (Judge/Jury) This is why the Taliban don't want to give him up. <BR>The only other way to get OBL, would be to have the trial in Europe. But it would have to be a U.N. war crimes trial. That way once found guilty, he would be shot within 24 hours.<P>Finally you need to find all the "cells". This is the most difficult part. Many terrorists are from many nations and have "hidden" themselves in many more. This is the biggest problem. If terrorist attack again, the "stereotyped" violence will increase. I don't think beating up a middle-east Gas station owner just because he "looks" middle-eastern. But the news portrays these terrorist as living among us. Since there is no way to tell the difference, The stereotypical violence grows.<P>As for the news, I am tired of them making a big deal over civilian casualties. Doesn't anyone wonder where the SAM missles that they shot at our planes fell at? You send a missle up to get a plane and when it misses, It has to fall somewhere. Shooting 120mm anti-aircraft shells into the air also will fall back down. The U.N. de-mining building real irked me. They were talking to one of the rescue people and the guy complained they may not get the bodies out until the next day. Well H&LL, How about helping us dig out our buildings. It's been over a month!! Everytime the Taliban complain about the "200 civilians" all I can think of is Gee, only 6800 more and were even. (sorry if that offends some folks but that is my feelings). <P>I have a feeling that this will be on-going for many years. There is no way out now. There will always be terrorists. The level of the fight will change but it'll always be there and be violent. If anyone thinks this will end in a year or two you are wrong. We will get rid of those who caused the Sept. 11 attacks but there will always be someone else who will step up and lead the oppressed.<BR>Oppressed and demoralized populations are highly susceptable to propoganda. They will always believe what the oppressors say. An abused child or spouse will always believe what the abuser says and defend them. It is the nature of the beast. Hitler found out how to manipulate the German people into backing him by using the same methods. The Taliban will be able to do it with the Afghanistan population too. Once the ground troops enter, the propoganda will flow and the Taliban will force us to kill civilians and use that against us. Our only hope is that someone else is able to manipulate the population. It has to be someone they know. Anyone else would be labeled the enemy. I am not sure if the Northern Alliance can do it.<BR>They need to work on getting the population behind them but they seem more pre-occupied with taking over useless Taliban military installations.<BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Gerald||10-15-2001||12:38 PM||||Woh on this planet could get OBL of the hook any way. I dont think jonny is up to it. Its blindingly obivous that he will be found guilty. As for the burning of the american flag by afgans and a simler act being carried out by americans. Does anyone even know what the afgan flag looks like? I for one have no idea. But when I find out I think I might use one to start a fire the next time me and my friends have a party. We can film ourselfs getting drunk and laughing about the bombings and send that over to them. Thats just my opinion.<p>[This message has been edited by Gerald (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Butter||10-15-2001||12:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Our only hope is that someone else is able to manipulate the population. It has to be someone they know. Anyone else would be labeled the enemy. I am not sure if the Northern Alliance can do it.<BR>They need to work on getting the population behind them but they seem more pre-occupied with taking over useless Taliban military installations.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>This is the KEY.  <BR>It just ain't the chicken hawk in the hen house.  That damn hawk's got the rooster's actin' like him too!  This makes for some bad eggs and pretty soon they'll have the hens believin' it too.  Seems the hawk has got the roosters fightin' roosters while he's cleanin' up the roosters and havin' some hen on the side.<P>Seems we need to kill the hawk and replace the bad roosters with good ones.  Now what does one do with all the roosters in question?  Well you check them out real good and if they don't pass inspection you fry em'.  This will put the farm back in order.<P>We must educate the ignorant carefully or you will wind up with educated idiots.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Doni Hagan||10-15-2001||03:12 PM||||Orief...<P>I just happened to turn on the radio and WLS-AM was reporting that an envelope containing Anthrax was sent to Senator Majority Leader Tom Dashell's office in DC. Although the head of the FBI has stated he doubts that Ben Laden has anything to do with it, President Bush just stated that he doesn't "put it past him."<P>Come on George...I voted for you. Like Public Enemy said "DON'T BELIEVE THE HYPE!!"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ken Wittlief||10-15-2001||05:29 PM||||best thing we can do to OBL and his followers is to make them quietly dissappear from the face of the earth.<P>No televised capture<P>no trial<P>no execution<P>no outcrys from the radical muslims<P>no 'dying for Islam in a Jihad'<P>no marterdom for him<P> our special forces quietly seek him out, and the messages and tapes from him stop coming - his remains are never found and he is never heard from again<P>  and 'we dont know what happened to him!'||||reg||1
Z||000026||Uaana||10-15-2001||07:00 PM||||Just my 2c on putting OSB on trial. <BR>For those who think this will show all those in the world that he really is a criminal and deserving of punishment. <P>Sorry you will never change their minds. <BR>If you think I'm wrong. Think back to the O.J. trial. That man could have been caught with the knife in his hand and thousands would have thought the evidence was planted because Furman once said something non-PC.||||reg||1
Z||000027||My7Fieros||10-15-2001||07:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>best thing we can do to OBL and his followers is to make them quietly dissappear from the face of the earth.<P>No televised capture<P>no trial<P>no execution<P>no outcrys from the radical muslims<P>no 'dying for Islam in a Jihad'<P>no marterdom for him<P> our special forces quietly seek him out, and the messages and tapes from him stop coming - his remains are never found and he is never heard from again<P>  and 'we dont know what happened to him!'</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That sounds like a good plan to me!<P>||||reg||14
