Z||000040||Raydar||10-17-2001||10:07 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>best thing we can do to OBL and his followers is to make them quietly dissappear from the face of the earth.<BR>No televised capture<P>no trial<P>no execution<P>no outcrys from the radical muslims<P>no 'dying for Islam in a Jihad'<P>no marterdom for him<P>our special forces quietly seek him out, and the messages and tapes from him stop coming - his remains are never found and he is never heard from again<P>and 'we dont know what happened to him!'<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ken, I agree completely. <P>Raydar<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||EdsB52||10-17-2001||12:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR>[B][/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"People like OBL WANT public attention - they want to be in the spot light - they WANT everyone to see their face on CNN."<P>Then why don't they turn themselves in so they can have more attention? Some criminals fall into that category; they want to get caught for the publicity so they purposely leave clues for the cops. Other cop to crimes they didn't commit for the attention. But Bin Laden and crew want to never get caught and will continue to seek attention through their evil deeds. If they were to get caught, they would make as big a circus out of it as possible, but they would hate it worse than death.<P>"If we catch him and put him on trial do you think he will be sulking around with his head hung in shame and embarrasment?!" <P>No, he would save that activity for his quiet time, you know, the 23 hours a day of solitary confinement.<P>"He will be ranting and raving the same old thing right up to the second they inject the lethal drug into his iv."<P>Sure he will; he will regardless. So are agreeing with deterrence as a means to stop heinous acts? Do you think killing rotten people deters other rottens from committing egregious acts? Sounds to me like you're refuting capital punishment, or for that matter most forms of punishment as not having deterrent value. Care to comment?<P>"and the terrorist acts will flare up again."<P>They will and currently are with all of the anthrax stuff going on. So who was saying we need to be careful not to falsely blame the good Arabs in our country? Let's see, maybe someone can help me out here; I wonder what the nationality is, of the people who are sending the anthrax, since it was mailed after the terrorists died? Did anyone hear about the Shiite (or sh!t) that claimed he was beaten up and had eggs thrown on him by white people? He was a student at ASU and claimed he was racially attacked. He provoked the emotion of many local residents (except me), so he did it again, and then again when the cops started to catch on to his game and cornered him. Well, like most punks, he wasn't too smooth and caved in to admit what he had done and charges are being filed. What he did was a form of domestic terrorism and he should be prosecuted as such, but probably will not. Not to be Archie Bunker, but I think the presumption should be that naturalized Arabs tend to have attributes that make them inclined to some form of terrorism. Notice I wrote, "naturalized?" I believe that most naturalized Americans from anywhere don't have the same respect for old glory that born Americans have. I've taken classes that deal with immigration and have had experiences with many people that have immigrated and reinforce my contention with those experiences.<P>"We need to change the subject. We need to focus on rebuilding afganistan, putting and end to the conflict between pakistand and india, and show the islamic world that we are not out to destroy islam - that america is founded on the principles of freedom, democracy, and personal responsibility."<P>Are you using "we" in the context of 'we in the forum,' or is it more like we in America? Because 'we' in the forum can write about what we want. But I agree with the rest of it. I think America should help that entire region to industrialize as soon as we remove their governments'.<P>"Finding a way for Israel to rebuild their temple, without having to tear down the dome of the rock, would be sign of goodwill on our part, and would resolve some of the tension between the muslims and the jews - both can co-exist in the same area - one does not have to give up anything for the other to have what is important too."<P>That's your non-Muslim opinion. I wish they could but don't think they will. If we can, that's great, but I don't think that'll happen. If they industrialize, their youth will begin to have money to spend on trendy things and move toward western ways, which will be a great place to start.||||reg||1
Z||000042||Tonker||10-17-2001||07:00 PM||||I think it would be best if OBL and his cronies "disappeared" during the action in Afghanistan. Following his disappearance, the US government should reveal the information they had linking OBL and his organisation to the terrorist attacks on the US.<P>While it would be nice to be able to "serve justice" by having a trial, it might be counter-productive. It gives OBL a highly visible platform for unleash his propaganda, and gives him a shot a martyrdom. Having him disappear, might do the same thing, but I think it less likely than if he were put on trial and executed or put in prison. <P>A public trial followed by a death sentence would be the worst outcome of all. Many muslims - even our allies -  are already skeptical about the action in the Middle East. They're unlikely to be swayed by a public trial. Putting him to death after that would certainly make him a martyr. Putting him on trial and parading him around, I think would have much the same outcome.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000043||EdsB52||10-17-2001||07:12 PM||||I would marvel in the public humiliation dealt to OBL though.<P>I noticed nobody wants to touch my deterrence point. All the while, many if not most people subscribe to deterrence theories, they just don't know why. I'll tell you why; it's a warm and fuzzy facade for the truth that what entices most people about punishment, especially capital punishment, is the revenge factor. But it's not civilized to profess revenge, so we call it deterrence.||||reg||1
Z||000044||maryjane||10-17-2001||08:07 PM||||Deterence. It only works on the normally law-abiding and rightous population. By rightous, I mean those of us who take our responsibilities seriously, and place the same value on other's lives that we do our own. People who have a lot to lose, either real or imagined, will shrink away from anything that might result in losing that which they value. Hardened criminals (my opinion only, I have no practical exp in this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ), are usually already down pretty much, so feel they can take a chance on bettering themselves. Figure it's no big loss if they get caught, since they are already doing without. Deterrence on a global scale worked with the mutually assured destruction concept, only because the results could be so horrible. I do not think the death penalty works in most cases as detterence, because the current and past couple generations have less 'moral' values, than , say the people of the '50s. Dettering terrorism would be difficult, because most feel that death for themselves, and even their families, would be preferable to their present condition. However, if the prospect of the entire country being demolished from the face of the Earth were realistic, then deterrence would be in effect. The only country that came close to that was Japan, and now has a constitution banning foriegn military involvement. Most of the generation that was alive at the end of WWII is either dead or very old, so I suspect their constitution may be ammended soon. I'm typing this hurridly, so hope it makes sense. No time for spell or grammar checking today.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000045||Mach10||10-17-2001||10:07 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> Eds on MY side?!??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000046||EdsB52||10-18-2001||03:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Don</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Deterence. It only works on the normally law-abiding and rightous population."<P>True by concept, but specifically not necessary due to the fact that law-abiding citizens don't need deterrence. Deterrence doesn’t work on career criminals and people that sporadically decide to commit crime since most people contemplating crime are under the assumption they won't be caught. And most of society doesn't need to be deterred from committing crime, as they wouldn't act criminally if it were allowed, so deterrence, for the most part, is a lonely concept that people use as misdirected rationalization to express other feelings. Now there are certain people in the fringe, especially young people, where deterrence can be used to modify their behavior, albeit few.<P>"People who have a lot to lose, either real or imagined, will shrink away from anything that might result in losing that which they value."<P>True to a degree, but there are exceptions and not necessarily few. OJ Simpson (presumably), Governor Mecham (Arizona), Governor Symington (Arizona), Osama Bin Laden, Ray Carruth (Panthers), Richard Nixon, Bill Clinton, and thousands of others in positions where they have it made, but are willing to risk it all. I use wealth and prestige as a barometer as well, but it's nowhere near 100%, as many people who are set are willing to risk it all to achieve some heinous goal. <P>"Hardened criminals (my opinion only, I have no practical exp in this   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ), are usually already down pretty much, so feel they can take a chance on bettering themselves. Figure it's no big loss if they get caught, since they are already doing without."<P>I agree 100% here, so punishing these guys is pure revenge, which might be suitable, but what irks me is when people are stupid or gullible enough to buy into and perpetuate the myth that general deterrence (watching someone else getting punished) dissuades others from committing crime. Let's call it revenge and go after them, I don't have a problem with that, but just be sure to tell your children that society is going to fry a convicted murderer so society can exact revenge. Don't be chicken and babble some deterrence crap, unless you also want them to believe in Santa Claus at 15 years old. Proof of my contention is that the crime rate is no different in states that employ capital punishment, versus the 12 states that don't (12 states plus others that have a moratorium on cap. pun.).<P>"I do not think the death penalty works in most cases as detterence, because the current and past couple generations have less 'moral' values, than , say the people of the '50s."<P>Come on Don, lets not pass moral judgements, as morality is a dynamic entitity that evolves through time. Not to mention the fact that morality arises and is somewhat controled by religious values. For example, open and notorious cohabitation isn't even statuted, except maybe in the bible belt, and even then it is never pursued as it would have been in the 1920's. Are you aware that a white female could have been deported in the early 1900's for marrying a non-white male? They were called anti-miscegination laws. I'll bet that generation considered the next generation (WWII generation) to be imoral for repealing those laws through Supreme Court rulings. Morality is a handy tool for coercion and it usually works in concert with any of the hundeds of world religions at the disposal of whomever wants to manipulate a group of people. Thank God for separation of church and state! Like my paradox? In my opinion, corporal punishment is extremely 'imoral,' and is now illegal, so I like many of the ways in which society is evolving.<P>"Deterrence on a global scale worked with the mutually assured destruction concept, only because the results could be so horrible."<P>You say 'worked,' are you referring to WWII? If so, there wasn't a lot of mutuality for the US. Let's not compare the destruction to Japan or Germany to that of Pearl Harbor. Maybe your theory worked in regard to the cold war, but that's about it as I see it. With many countries possessing nuclear weapons, a degree of that is present. To support your contention on a local basis, i would assert an armed society is a polite society.<P>"Dettering terrorism would be difficult, because most feel that death for themselves, and even their families, would be preferable to their present condition."<P>True true. Deterrence is an empty concept that fools use to disguise other agendas.<P>"However, if the prospect of the entire country being demolished from the face of the Earth were realistic, then deterrence would be in effect."<P>Maybe for some, maybe for none; it would be purely situational. If you introduce Nazi Germany, it's hard to support your contention. And Japan didn't surrender until long after they were almost completely demolished. If you introduce some of the Middle Eastern countries that have waffled onto our side, your argument has some merit.<P>"The only country that came close to that was Japan, and now has a constitution banning foriegn military involvement. Most of the generation that was alive at the end of WWII is either dead or very old, so I suspect their constitution may be ammended soon."<P>I didn't know that they had a Constitution banning foreign military involvement. Other countries don't have to honor their Constitution, so it's kind of a worthless piece of paper. Maybe you mean that in the context that they won't assist other governments in military exercises; please expound.||||reg||1
Z||000047||maryjane||10-18-2001||11:21 PM||||In reference to law abiding citizens being deterred from crime, I think that is more accurate than thought. Maybe not in x-y generation, but in older gens, it is still considered a huge embarrassment and disgrace to be arrested, even for small offenses.<BR> When I speak of those who have a lot to lose, I refer to the general population, specifically people who have families who depend on them. I think, tho, that the more affluent a person is, this concept decays somewhat. It's not that they are afraid of being caught, they believe they are either above the law, or can afford to beat it, even if guilty.<BR>MAD, has only been viable since the emergence of global nuclear arsenals by several powers.<BR>Nuclear destruction of a country would destroy much more than the military and civilian infrastructure. The potential long term consequences hanging over future generations would tend to take the fight out of most any society. This was not true in WWII, in Germany or Japan. They both still had the will to fight to the end, because both felt that there would be a beginning even after a defeat. In other words, their society would survive, they felt, as it had in wars and defeats of past centuries, even after the firebombings of Tokyo and Dresden. Faced with possibly complete nuclear annihilation, that survival becomes questionable and not worth the risk-hence- deterrence.<BR>Article 9 of the Japanese Constitution states:<BR>"(1) Aspiring sincerely to an international peace based on justice and order, the Japanese people forever renounce war as a sovereign right of the nation and the threat or use of force as a means of settling international disputes.<BR>(2) In order to accomplish the aim of the preceding paragraph, land, sea, and air forces, as well as other war potential, will never be maintained.<BR>(3) The right of aggression of the State will not be recognized."<BR>This has been amended to allow for homeland defense and to allow for participation of Japan's forces in UN peacekeeping (A joke),<BR>but even today, Japan has no long range bombers, missiles, submarines or any other military force capable of projecting power outside it's immediate geographical area. (Unless Godzilla attacks) This constitution was, more or less, based on an old 1899 constitution, but with the above and a few other changes that were added and insisted upon by Supreme Allied Commander (Mac) at the end of WWII.<BR>||||reg||1
